avfilter/vf_yadif_videotoolbox: one command buffer per frame

This is FFmpeg PR 26244 as reviewed upstream (head 73b7b4496b), ported to
n8.1.3, plus one fix that upstream gets with the planned factoring of
both VideoToolbox deinterlacers. n8.1.3's YADIFContext.filter returns
void, where master returns the error since 2b99c068db, so filter() wraps
the upstream body (filter_planes). A failed frame still goes out, so the
wrapper gives it the attachments and field state a successful one gets.

The added fix: the output pool takes the input frames context's range,
or the negotiated one when that is unspecified, as bwdif_videotoolbox
does. Behind hwupload the range is unspecified, so full-range NV12 came
out in a video-range (420v) buffer.

The filter committed a command buffer and waited for it once per plane,
so an NV12 or P010 frame went to the GPU twice and the calling thread
waited twice. The planes are now dispatches on one compute encoder in one
command buffer, with one commit and one wait per frame. The parameters go
by value through setBytes (iOS 8.3), so the shared parameter buffer is
gone: two planes in one submission could not share it.

The output is unchanged. framemd5 of NV12 and P010, send_field and
send_frame, TFF and BFF, at 160x120, 1920x1080 and 38x10 matches the
previous FFmpegBuild patch frame for frame (360 frames). On an M1, 1200
fields of 1080p59.94 took 2.34 ms per field before and 1.86 ms after.

Fixes that ride along, because the rewrite owns these lines:

- commandBuffer and computeCommandEncoder return autoreleased (+0)
  objects, and FFmpeg builds Objective-C without ARC, but n8.1.3 also
  released them by hand. That over-release crashes a host app whose GCD
  queue pops its autorelease pool when the work block ends. The per-frame
  @autoreleasepool is now their only release (upstream since 7f2e5cdd7c).
- A texture the texture cache fails to create stops the frame instead of
  going into CFRelease as NULL. A missing command buffer or encoder and a
  command buffer that ends in error are logged.
- The output pool hands out the same pixel buffer again, and
  CVBufferPropagateAttachments only adds keys, so the output's attachments
  are cleared first. Before, an HLG transfer function from one frame
  stayed on every later untagged frame.
- The output is progressive, so the field count, field detail and
  bottom-field chroma location attachments are dropped.
- The unused kernel parameters are gone.

Proposed in AetherEngine issue 1101.
